WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.530
The planet is Arrakis.

00:00:02.530 --> 00:00:03.790
Also known as Dune.

00:00:03.790 --> 00:00:05.839
And y’all, it’s a mess.

00:00:05.839 --> 00:00:10.280
December of this year, we were supposed to
see the arrival of director Denis Villeneuve’s

00:00:10.280 --> 00:00:16.910
interpretation of the 1965 novel Dune, which
had been previously (and rather infamously)

00:00:16.910 --> 00:00:22.760
brought to life by David Lynch in 1984, and
again in a three part miniseries on the SyFy

00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:24.650
channel in the early 2000s.

00:00:24.650 --> 00:00:30.289
Now many sci-fi nerds (including myself) were
both excited and nervous about the new adaptation

00:00:30.289 --> 00:00:36.100
directed by Villeneuve, but owing to the ongoing
plague of eternity, the release has been pushed

00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:39.040
back to next year.

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:43.500
So in lieu of that, y’all have to use this
video to tide you over.

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:44.810
What is Dune?

00:00:44.810 --> 00:00:46.690
Why must the spice flow?

00:00:46.690 --> 00:00:48.400
And what is with all the sand?

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:56.030
(I hate sand, it’s coarse and it gets everywhere)

00:00:56.030 --> 00:01:02.120
Dune is a 1965 science-fiction novel by American
author Frank Herbert, and is the first installment

00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:07.830
of the Dune Saga, which is made up of five
sequels written by Herbert himself and several

00:01:07.830 --> 00:01:12.790
subsequent books made under the collaboration
of Brian Herbert (Frank Herbert’s son) and

00:01:12.790 --> 00:01:15.010
writer Kevin J. Anderson.

00:01:15.010 --> 00:01:17.620
They are … controversial.

00:01:17.620 --> 00:01:21.870
While not an instant bestseller, it was a
massive critical success, with winning both

00:01:21.870 --> 00:01:25.610
the Hugo and the Nebula awards in 1966.

00:01:25.610 --> 00:01:29.360
Dune itself would eventually be called the
“best selling science fiction book of all

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:31.610
time” in 2003.

00:01:31.610 --> 00:01:37.220
Taking place in humanity’s distant future,
the universe is now a feudal interstellar

00:01:37.220 --> 00:01:43.350
society in which various noble houses control
planetary fiefs, with the most important being

00:01:43.350 --> 00:01:45.030
the desert planet Arrakis.

00:01:45.030 --> 00:01:51.810
Ya see, Arrakis is the source of the spice
melange which extends your life, gives you

00:01:51.810 --> 00:01:55.729
cool mental abilities, and is essential for
space navigation.

00:01:55.729 --> 00:01:58.790
And it absolutely must flow.

00:01:58.790 --> 00:02:04.390
You’d think with a planet that important
it would just belong to everyone … but … I

00:02:04.390 --> 00:02:06.000
guess they're going for realism

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:12.160
The current galactic ruler, the Emperor Shaddam
IV (4th) is what we would call, an insecure

00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:13.160
hater.

00:02:13.160 --> 00:02:18.150
He sees how popular this one guy, Duke Leto
Atreides, has gotten and takes advantage of

00:02:18.150 --> 00:02:24.410
a blood feud between House Atreides and their
rivals, House Harkonnen, to destroy the Atreides

00:02:24.410 --> 00:02:25.410
family.

00:02:25.410 --> 00:02:30.200
And so Arrakis, which previously belonged
to the Harkonnens, is given to Duke Leto---with

00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:37.120
the intention that he and his family have
move to this sandy, mostly inhospitable planet

00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:39.620
in order to protect the spice melange.

00:02:39.620 --> 00:02:41.150
What could go wrong!?!

00:02:41.150 --> 00:02:43.670
Enter Paul.

00:02:43.670 --> 00:02:49.650
Paul is the heir of House Atreides and at
the ripe age of 15 he is ready to be the protagonist

00:02:49.650 --> 00:02:51.970
of a science fiction series.

00:02:51.970 --> 00:02:55.240
Paul is like the chosen one over 9000.

00:02:55.240 --> 00:02:56.680
Prophetic visions?

00:02:56.680 --> 00:02:57.680
Yup.

00:02:57.680 --> 00:02:58.780
Super rich?

00:02:58.780 --> 00:02:59.780
Yes.

00:02:59.780 --> 00:03:01.880
Heir of a tragic family?

00:03:01.880 --> 00:03:03.130
Yes.

00:03:03.130 --> 00:03:08.160
Superpowered child as the result of a centuries
long breeding program and positioned to be

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:11.820
a prophet to a group of very specifically
coded sand-people?

00:03:11.820 --> 00:03:15.650
Specific, but yes.

00:03:15.650 --> 00:03:18.770
Spoilers from this point on by the way.

00:03:18.770 --> 00:03:24.050
There is an ambush and Paul’s dad is captured,
but he kills himself using a cyanide capsule.

00:03:24.050 --> 00:03:29.510
Paul and his mother, Jessica, meet up with
the Fremean, the native population of Dune.

00:03:29.510 --> 00:03:34.550
They see Paul as a prophet, and he takes on
the Fremean name Paul Muad'Dib.

00:03:34.550 --> 00:03:40.260
After training, getting a new stabby younger
sister, a girlfriend named Chani and taming

00:03:40.260 --> 00:03:47.849
the sandworms (massive nightmare snakes that
live in the wild and from whose poop spice

00:03:47.849 --> 00:03:53.099
is made), Paul ends up getting revenge for
the death of his father.

00:03:53.099 --> 00:04:00.940
After he defeats the Baron Harkonnen (who
is also his surprise grandfather), Paul requests

00:04:00.940 --> 00:04:05.750
an audience with Shaddam IV and threatens
to destroy the spice melange, unless he gets

00:04:05.750 --> 00:04:11.129
to marry the Emperor's daughter, Princess
Irulan and the Emperor abdicates in favor

00:04:11.129 --> 00:04:13.970
of Paul, who is, I reiterate, is a teenager.

00:04:13.970 --> 00:04:15.040
Yay?

00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:19.609
And that is like the super spark notes summary.

00:04:19.609 --> 00:04:25.190
What makes the first book in the saga so compelling
is how it sets up all these themes that Frank

00:04:25.190 --> 00:04:27.430
Herbert later deconstructs.

00:04:27.430 --> 00:04:32.849
Franklin Patrick Herbert Jr., noted Libra,
had been a pulp writer, photographer, and

00:04:32.849 --> 00:04:37.520
journalist before he started doing research
for Dune in 1959.

00:04:37.520 --> 00:04:42.710
The endeavor took him six years and more than
400 pages of writing to complete---making

00:04:42.710 --> 00:04:45.830
Dune much longer than most science fiction
at the time.

00:04:45.830 --> 00:04:51.309
Kevin Mulcahy in “The Prince on Arrakis:
Frank Herbert's dialogue with Machiavelli”

00:04:51.309 --> 00:04:52.309
explains that

00:04:52.309 --> 00:04:55.229
“Herbert was writing at the height of the
Cold War.

00:04:55.229 --> 00:05:00.069
From the conception of Dune to its publication,
a presidential campaign centered around fears

00:05:00.069 --> 00:05:06.039
of a "missile gap"; [...] Read in their historical
context, Dune and (to a lesser extent) its

00:05:06.039 --> 00:05:09.009
sequels are subversive texts.[...].

00:05:09.009 --> 00:05:15.289
Herbert dramatizes in Dune an unpleasant political
truth: people cannot free themselves of Machiavellian

00:05:15.289 --> 00:05:20.699
leaders until they first free themselves of
their desire to be led.”

00:05:20.699 --> 00:05:25.931
Jennifer Simkins, an English teacher in New
South Wales, Australia, also points out that

00:05:25.931 --> 00:05:31.479
the Dune novels “suggest that blind acceptance
of social structures, and the mythic patterns

00:05:31.479 --> 00:05:36.789
that inform them, leads to the intellectual
stagnation of humankind.

00:05:36.789 --> 00:05:44.300
[...] For Herbert, SF is a mode that “helps
us understand what it means to be human”

00:05:44.300 --> 00:05:46.310
by examining established myths.”

00:05:46.310 --> 00:05:50.339
The Dune saga highlights Herbert’s evergoing
concern with leadership.

00:05:50.339 --> 00:05:56.169
He had always expressed that the first book
and the character of Paul was meant to examine

00:05:56.169 --> 00:06:00.509
human tendency to follow charismatic leaders.

00:06:00.509 --> 00:06:04.949
Except you don’t particularly get that element
until the second book Dune Messiah, which

00:06:04.949 --> 00:06:06.879
puts the D in deconstruction.

00:06:06.879 --> 00:06:12.779
You see, in the first book, Paul hijacks the
Fremen’s religion in order to get power

00:06:12.779 --> 00:06:17.939
and revenge, which is like yay white boy power
fantasy pew pew.

00:06:17.939 --> 00:06:21.720
However, it soon becomes a power that Paul
cannot control.

00:06:21.720 --> 00:06:26.710
As a result, when we open Dune Messiah, we
are told that the Fremen started a jihad in

00:06:26.710 --> 00:06:32.979
Paul's name across the galaxy that kills (flips
book open) sixty billion people across the

00:06:32.979 --> 00:06:36.169
known universe.

00:06:36.169 --> 00:06:38.599
Colonization is bad, m’kay.

00:06:38.599 --> 00:06:44.331
One of Paul’s visions that he gets from
being special, is that the future of humanity

00:06:44.331 --> 00:06:47.969
could go down a path where it will stagnate
and destroy itself.

00:06:47.969 --> 00:06:53.779
Thus, Paul thinks that the only way to prevent
this future of eventual human extinction is

00:06:53.779 --> 00:07:00.870
to engender such oppressive horrible leadership
that humanity will never follow a leader again.

00:07:00.870 --> 00:07:03.139
Sounds legit.

00:07:03.139 --> 00:07:08.639
His not official wife, Chani, dies leaving
behind a pair of chosen one TWINS named Luke

00:07:08.639 --> 00:07:13.629
and Leia … ahem, I mean Leto II and Ghanima.

00:07:13.629 --> 00:07:18.699
And when this happens, he goes into a state
of despair and pretty much abdicates his power

00:07:18.699 --> 00:07:20.759
because he can’t be full Vader.

00:07:20.759 --> 00:07:26.319
Also, that is shade for Star Wars not Dune
just so we're clear.

00:07:26.319 --> 00:07:31.939
Children of Dune, the third book in what we
could call the first Dune trilogy, sets up

00:07:31.939 --> 00:07:37.849
the next phase of the series with a new “protagonist”
in Paul’s son, Leto II.

00:07:37.849 --> 00:07:42.860
Both of his children have similar powers to
him, but it is Leto who is destined to become

00:07:42.860 --> 00:07:49.740
the God Emperor and he does so by being as
evil as he needs to be in order to ensure

00:07:49.740 --> 00:07:51.580
the survival of the world.

00:07:51.580 --> 00:07:57.050
So let's talk about Paul, the “hero” of
our story, at least at first.

00:07:57.050 --> 00:08:02.709
One of the things that is often debated is
if Paul would fall under what you’d call

00:08:02.709 --> 00:08:04.909
a “white savior” character.

00:08:04.909 --> 00:08:10.470
In many genres, including science fiction,
there is this trope of a white (or white adjacent

00:08:10.470 --> 00:08:17.689
person) coming into Black or iindigenous communities
(or in this case, black and indigenous coded),

00:08:17.689 --> 00:08:22.379
and becoming their leader by doing what they
already do, but better.

00:08:22.379 --> 00:08:23.419
Paul is both.

00:08:23.419 --> 00:08:27.820
A “white savior” played straight in the
original Dune, but a deconstruction in the

00:08:27.820 --> 00:08:28.919
later books.

00:08:28.919 --> 00:08:33.370
In Dune Messiah he compares himself to Hilter
at one point saying: “He killed more than

00:08:33.370 --> 00:08:34.700
six million.

00:08:34.700 --> 00:08:36.330
Pretty good for those days...

00:08:36.330 --> 00:08:42.150
Statistics: at a conservative estimate, I've
killed sixty-one billion, sterilized ninety

00:08:42.150 --> 00:08:45.410
planets, completely demoralized five hundred
others.

00:08:45.410 --> 00:08:48.600
I've wiped out the followers of forty religions…”

00:08:48.600 --> 00:08:54.400
Everything about his character is set up to
be this noble hero who wants to do good, but

00:08:54.400 --> 00:08:56.650
his visions are a trap.

00:08:56.650 --> 00:09:01.870
Paul does not want to subject the world to
living under a human god and that is criticized

00:09:01.870 --> 00:09:07.740
by some in text as being weak, but often,
as is the case with Dune especially, just

00:09:07.740 --> 00:09:12.600
because the text says one thing doesn’t
mean we are meant to take it as such.

00:09:12.600 --> 00:09:18.440
Especially considering that his son ends up
turning into a giant human/sandworm hybrid

00:09:18.440 --> 00:09:21.800
with only a human face and two t-rex arms.

00:09:21.800 --> 00:09:26.750
This physical transformation highlights a
point brought up again in Mulcahy’s, piece

00:09:26.750 --> 00:09:33.070
“Herbert's Dune novels demonstrate the inevitable
consequences of political manipulation [...[ One

00:09:33.070 --> 00:09:39.190
cannot use others without being tainted; people
cannot accede to their own exploitation without

00:09:39.190 --> 00:09:41.840
surrendering some of their humanity.”

00:09:41.840 --> 00:09:47.170
Paul’s fate is ultimately to be killed by
one of his own acolytes who doesn’t recognize

00:09:47.170 --> 00:09:49.960
him--a kind of poetic irony.

00:09:49.960 --> 00:09:51.690
Simkins later explains that:

00:09:51.690 --> 00:09:57.500
“The depiction of Paul in Dune as a stereotypical
hero activates the reader’s awareness of

00:09:57.500 --> 00:10:04.130
the hero schema before the dangers of absolute
rule are illuminated in the sequels.

00:10:04.130 --> 00:10:09.510
Herbert explains: “Dune was set up to imprint
on you the reader, a superhero.

00:10:09.510 --> 00:10:10.910
I wanted you…

00:10:10.910 --> 00:10:13.470
totally involved with that superhero…

00:10:13.470 --> 00:10:16.280
And then I wanted to show what happens”

00:10:16.280 --> 00:10:22.190
Herbert is both addressing this imperialistic
mindset, but also using it as the basis of

00:10:22.190 --> 00:10:27.910
a plan that is supposed to lead the world
into “salvation”, all the while asking

00:10:27.910 --> 00:10:30.580
if any of this maneuvering is worth it.

00:10:30.580 --> 00:10:34.990
Would things have ended differently if Paul
had just gotten revenge for his father’s

00:10:34.990 --> 00:10:36.940
death and left it there?

00:10:36.940 --> 00:10:41.670
In my opinion, I think that Herbert is using
Paul’s story to show how these ideas are

00:10:41.670 --> 00:10:47.790
toxic and corrosive, with Leto’s transformation
being a physical manifestation of that.

00:10:47.790 --> 00:10:56.420
I mean Leto’s plan is to repress humanity
for thousands of years, so that when he dies,

00:10:56.420 --> 00:10:59.070
they yeet into the stars and beyond.

00:10:59.070 --> 00:11:04.870
Which is basically the same plan as a parent
in a fairytale who puts their child in a tower

00:11:04.870 --> 00:11:10.220
rather than just say “hey don’t touch
spinning wheels.”

00:11:10.220 --> 00:11:14.610
Or it's like how, after WWII ended, everyone
looked at each other and all agreed, "let's

00:11:14.610 --> 00:11:17.690
never have an authoritarian again."

00:11:17.690 --> 00:11:18.690
Yeah.

00:11:18.690 --> 00:11:23.270
There are also still issues with the way that
the Fremen are used and they become weapons.

00:11:23.270 --> 00:11:29.030
Because, despite the text being critical of
that—it doesn’t give them any more humanity.

00:11:29.030 --> 00:11:30.950
They remain cyphers.

00:11:30.950 --> 00:11:36.140
I think David M. Higgins, Speculative Fiction
Editor for the Los Angeles Review of Books,

00:11:36.140 --> 00:11:41.030
put it best, although a tad bit harsher than
me, when he said:

00:11:41.030 --> 00:11:46.040
“...Herbert essentially offers a critique
of territorial imperialism while upholding

00:11:46.040 --> 00:11:52.230
the axioms of capitalist accumulation that
motivate modern imperial exploits in the first

00:11:52.230 --> 00:11:57.390
place[…] Herbert offers a Western audience
a way to have their cake and eat it too: readers

00:11:57.390 --> 00:12:04.280
are invited to distance themselves from identification
with imperial oppressors and to identify with

00:12:04.280 --> 00:12:09.140
decolonizing nationalists overcoming the tyranny
of imperialism.

00:12:09.140 --> 00:12:10.140
Tea.

00:12:10.140 --> 00:12:15.180
Dune is hard to adapt because it is just the
beginning of a much larger story.

00:12:15.180 --> 00:12:19.580
We have only touched on the first three books
in this video and it still has encompassed

00:12:19.580 --> 00:12:23.010
enough for a whole semester of Philosophy
101.

00:12:23.010 --> 00:12:29.380
A filmmaker who sees Paul as a savior can’t
really comprehend the fullness of the story.

00:12:29.380 --> 00:12:32.300
In some ways that is the trap of Dune.

00:12:32.300 --> 00:12:37.300
Only seeing the surface level entertainment
that is meant to deliver these intense messages:

00:12:37.300 --> 00:12:42.060
As Herbert himself once said: “If you want
to get anything across, you have to be entertaining

00:12:42.060 --> 00:12:43.060
first.

00:12:43.060 --> 00:12:45.950
If you start standing on a street corner,
people will tune you out.

00:12:45.950 --> 00:12:51.040
[...]But analogues give you a marvelous device
for getting past that screening system, because

00:12:51.040 --> 00:12:56.090
people can be caught up in the drama of the
story, be deep into the problems of it.

00:12:56.090 --> 00:13:01.410
Then later on, much later on, they say, Oh,
my God, he was talking about this!"

00:13:01.410 --> 00:13:05.250
And they come out of it with a brand new view
of what's happening in their world.”

00:13:05.250 --> 00:13:07.190
At least you hope so anyway.

00:13:07.190 --> 00:13:12.470
Dune is an iconic work of science-fiction
and while we should be critical of it and

00:13:12.470 --> 00:13:17.630
discuss the ways in which it is dated, there
is no denying that it has a huge cultural

00:13:17.630 --> 00:13:23.140
impact on the genre and asked some really
compelling questions, even if it didn’t

00:13:23.140 --> 00:13:25.740
always deliver on the answers.

00:13:25.740 --> 00:13:32.470
The spice … must flow.

